            <ArticleTitle>Intraosseous Angiolipoma of the Rib.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1409-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s12262-014-1147-x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Angiolipomas are a subgroup of lipomas and are rarely observed benign tumours in which mature fat tissue and angiomatous elements exist jointly. Costa-induced angiolipomas are rarer. A male patient aged 46 years consulted our clinic due to severe pain in his left side. The patient's X-ray showed a lobulated area with increased density (bone mass) in the left upper zone of the lung. A lesion in the fourth rib was excised with a left thoracotomy. Pathological diagnosis was reported as angiolipoma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
